<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Rhandeia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/62">62</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-parthian-war-of-58-63">Roman–Parthian War of 58–63</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Rhandeia was fought in 62 CE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> during the war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> over the Armenian throne. A Roman army under Lucius Caesennius Paetus was besieged by the Parthian king Vologases I at Rhandeia and forced to accept humiliating terms. The settlement that followed in 63 let the Parthian prince Tiridates rule Armenia but receive his crown from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> in Rome.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-rhandeia.jpg" alt="Battle of Rhandeia" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman-Parthian_War_61-63.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 51 CE a Parthian campaign deposed Mithridates, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s client king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and installed his brother Tiridates I in his place. Rome would not accept a weakened eastern border, and in 58 Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo was sent with three legions; after a one-year campaign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> set Tigranes VI on the throne. In 61 Tigranes made an ill-advised probing attack into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, and Vologases I answered by besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranocerta">Tigranocerta</a>. The city was well supplied and its attackers were not, so both sides agreed to withdraw.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After a Parthian declaration of war, Lucius Caesennius Paetus, governor of Cappadocia, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 62 with only two legions, IV Scythica and XII Fulminata, hoping to make a military reputation. He took only a few minor forts and lack of supplies drove him west for the winter. Corbulo, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> strengthening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> frontier, did not march at once, and some accused him of waiting to win more glory from a rescue. When the calls for help came he set out with half the Syrian army and provisions on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, but before he arrived Paetus had been defeated by the Armenian and Parthian army near Rhandeia, northeast of Melitene, and had surrendered.</p><p>The terms were humiliating. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, give up their forts and build a bridge over the Arsanias so that Vologases could cross in triumph on an elephant. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> plundered the army of weapons and clothing, and according to rumours reported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> the Romans were made to pass under the yoke.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The two Roman forces met on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> near Melitene amid mutual grief. Paetus urged a new invasion, but Corbulo refused, saying he lacked the authority and that the troops were worn out. Envoys of Vologases then asked Corbulo to give up his bridgehead over the Euphrates, and he demanded the Parthian evacuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in turn. Both sides withdrew, leaving Armenia nominally independent but in practice under Parthian control until a Parthian delegation could travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The later treaty, signed at Rhandeia itself, made Tiridates king and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsacid-dynasty">Arsacid dynasty</a> of Armenia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Rhandeia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Arsacid branch took hold when Tiridates I, brother of the Parthian king, was crowned by Nero in Rome in 66 CE, a compromise that followed the Roman defeat at <b>Rhandeia</b> and let both empires claim a stake.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li></ul></section>
